## MCP Tools

mtix exposes tools via MCP (Model Context Protocol). Use the `mcp__mtix__` prefixed tools when available — they are preferred over CLI commands:

| Tool | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_context` | **MANDATORY** — assemble full context chain before any work |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_ready` | Find available work |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_claim` | Claim a node |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_done` | Mark work complete |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_show` | View node details |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_create` | Create a new node |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_decompose` | Batch create children |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_update` | Update node fields |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_search` | Full-text search |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_briefing` | **List nodes in briefing format** — labeled text blocks for context. Use instead of writing stub code to parse mtix_list output. Accepts multi-value filters (`under`, `status`, `type`, `fields`). |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_annotate` | Add traceability comment |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_session_start` | Start work session |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_session_end` | End work session |
| `mcp__mtix__mtix_agent_heartbeat` | Send heartbeat (every 5 min) |

## Key Rules

1. **Always call `mcp__mtix__mtix_context` before doing any work** — the assembled prompt is your complete briefing
2. Register with `mtix agent register <agent-id>` at session boot; repeats exit 0 with an "already registered" notice. Start a session before work and end it when done
3. Claim nodes before working on them
4. Follow the state machine — no shortcuts
5. Send heartbeats every 5 minutes to avoid stale detection
6. **Never leave tasks empty** — after `create` or `decompose`, every task MUST have `description`, `prompt`, and `acceptance` populated
7. Add a traceability comment before marking done: task → requirement → test → result
8. No stub implementations — functions must perform their documented purpose end-to-end
9. **Run `mtix sync` before you commit or push `.mtix/tasks.json`** — MCP tool results do not show the auto-import notices, so a write can succeed while the board on disk is a pulled one mtix has not imported. Commit it only when `Last auto-import refusal` does not read `pending`; otherwise follow the "Auto-import of .mtix/tasks.json Refused" procedure in [AGENTS.md]({{ .DocsPath }}AGENTS.md)

## Best Practices for Claude

- **Prefer MCP tools over CLI** when both are available — MCP tools return structured data
- **Batch operations** — use `mcp__mtix__mtix_decompose` instead of multiple `mcp__mtix__mtix_create` calls
- **Search before creating** — use `mcp__mtix__mtix_search` to check for existing similar tasks
- **Use briefing format for analysis** — call `mcp__mtix__mtix_briefing` with multi-value filters instead of writing Python/jq post-processing code. Example: `mtix_briefing(under="PROJ-1,PROJ-2", status="done", type="issue", fields="id,title,prompt")`
- **Progress checks** — use `mcp__mtix__mtix_progress` to verify rollup before reporting completion

## Watching Task Events

WebSocket subscriptions at `/ws/events` match `under` to the named node and its
dotted descendants on a `.` boundary: `PROJ-1` includes `PROJ-1` and `PROJ-1.2`,
but rejects `PROJ-10` and `PROJ-10.2`. Omitted or empty `under` accepts every node.
A non-empty `events` whitelist must also match; an omitted or empty `events`
list accepts every event type.

## Sync

{{ template "sync_section" . }}

Mirror lock contention saves the task change and leaves a durable pending export. Service import lock release, automatic export and daemon ticks retry after restart, with at most three successful passes per trigger; errors, contention and pulled-board refusals stop recovery. Long-running post-commit hooks mark before debounce, but the small commit-to-hook and CLI post-run windows remain. Metadata errors are reported while protected publication is attempted; unreadable snapshots retain requests. Retries protect changed, unreadable or refused pulled boards. Unix directory Sync EINVAL/ENOTSUP is logged as best-effort, and Windows does not sync directory entries; unsupported directory Sync carries no power-loss guarantee. Follow `mtix sync` import guidance and never edit pending request files by hand.

## Sync Hub Privileges

A `hub-privileges` **WARN** from `mtix sync doctor` is acceptable for everyday use (it may be fine on a private network) and blocks nothing. To restrict who can use the hub's sync tables, the table owner runs `mtix sync harden` (a dry run). **Never** run `mtix sync harden --apply` until the user has approved the dry run's role list, and never run `mtix sync harden` from a hook, a push or the daemon.

## Error Handling

If you encounter an error, check [TROUBLESHOOTING.md]({{ .DocsPath }}TROUBLESHOOTING.md) for common solutions. If a command prints `mtix: auto-import of .mtix/tasks.json refused`, nothing was imported: never run the `--mode replace` import it names just to silence it, because that deletes the comments, activity and tasks it lists and needs the ticket count typed at an interactive terminal, it cannot run unattended; follow the "Auto-import of .mtix/tasks.json Refused" procedure in [AGENTS.md]({{ .DocsPath }}AGENTS.md). If blocked, record the reason with `mtix_annotate`, then call `mtix_defer` (with an optional `until` wake time) rather than abandoning the task. Always document anomalies with `mtix_annotate`.

If `mtix sync doctor` fails its `quarantined events` check, `mtix sync pull` has held pulled events it could not validate or apply; every pull retries them. Run `mtix sync pull` again; if events remain, list them with `mtix sync quarantine list` (read-only) and give the operator the event ids and reasons. **Never** delete quarantine rows, edit the local database, or raise `sync.max_lamport_jump` without the user's confirmation.

If a command prints `WARN: ... over the 65536-byte sync limit`, or `mtix sync doctor` fails its `held push events` check, `mtix sync push` holds events the hub would refuse (and every change of the subtree of a task whose creation is held, and every dependency link or unlink made while a task creation is held) and pushes the rest. Follow the fix doctor names for each held event: shorten or split a field edit and save it again; for a held task creation, stop editing that task and tell the operator; a dependent resolves with the held creation it depends on, and a held link pushes once no creation made before it is held; a clock hold needs this machine's clock checked. **Never** delete quarantine rows or use `mtix sync reconcile --discard-local` (it deletes local tasks and unpushed changes, needs `mtix sync push` and `pending` 0 first, and asks for the ticket count at an interactive terminal) to clear held events.

If `mtix sync status` shows a `pending` count that does not go down across pushes, or `mtix sync doctor` fails its `queue draining` check, run `mtix sync push`: it acknowledges the events an earlier push already delivered (a lost confirmation, a crash before the local mark), counts them as `already on the hub` next to `inserted` in its output, and pushes the rest; `pending` then reaches 0 apart from held push events. **Never** mark events pushed by hand or discard them to make the queue drain.

`mtix sync clone` refuses, naming the event and writing nothing, while the hub holds an event that fails these checks: on the fresh store, recover with `mtix sync pull` alone (it quarantines the event), never any other way. **Never** run `mtix sync reconcile --discard-local` on your own: it deletes local tasks and unpushed changes and needs the ticket count typed at an interactive terminal (no flag supplies it), so it cannot run unattended; ask the user to run it, and only after `mtix sync push` and a `mtix sync status` showing pending 0.

Operator-local state: `mtix hooks status --json` validates its path and access rules without creating state or granting approval. Follow the admin skill for recovery; never commit or dotfile-sync operator-local state.

